p(32,3,"Alfred Smirk considered himself very handsome; he spent a great deal of time",
 "Alfred Smirk is vain and lazy and does not clean the box. The smell and fumes make the horse's eyes sore and take away his wish to eat.",
 "Alfred Smirk thought he was very handsome. He spent a great deal of time on his hair, whiskers and necktie, in front of a little looking-glass in the harness-room. When his master was speaking to him, it was always, “Yes, sir; yes, sir,” and he touched his hat at every word. Everyone thought he was a very nice young man and that Mr. Barry was lucky to find him. I would say that he was the laziest and most proud man I ever came near. Of course it was a great thing not to be treated badly, but a horse needs more than that. I had a loose box and might have been very comfortable if he had not been too lazy to clean it out. He never took all the straw away, and the smell from what lay underneath was very bad. The strong gases that rose up made my eyes sore and red, and I did not feel the same wish to eat my food.",
 ["conceited","indolent","vapors","inflame","appetite","fortunate"])
